# Audio-Based Book Summarization System
### Architecture: BART · Dataset: CMU Book Summary · Framework: Hugging Face

---

## Overview

This system implements the **BART** (Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformers)
architecture proposed by Lewis et al. (ACL 2020) to perform **abstractive book
summarization**. Given a book's plot/description, the model generates a concise,
fluent summary — which can optionally be converted to speech.

```
Book Text  ──►  BART Encoder (bidirectional)  ──►  BART Decoder (autoregressive)
                        │                                    │
                 Contextual representations          Beam-search decoding
                                                            │
                                                    Abstractive Summary
                                                            │
                                               [Optional] Text-to-Speech (MP3)
```

---

## Architecture Choices (from the paper)

| Component | Choice | Why |
|-----------|--------|-----|
| Pre-training | BART (text infilling + sentence permutation) | Best ROUGE on summarization |
| Encoder | Bidirectional Transformer (12 layers) | Captures full context |
| Decoder | Auto-regressive left-to-right (12 layers) | Fluent generation |
| Fine-tuning | Seq2Seq with label-smoothed cross-entropy | Matches BART paper §3.3 |
| Decoding | Beam search (k=4), length penalty=2.0, no-repeat-3gram | Standard for summarization |
| Evaluation | ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L | Standard summarization metrics |

---

## Project Structure

```
book_summarizer/
├── train.py            ← Full training pipeline
├── inference.py        ← Summarization + optional TTS
├── evaluate_model.py   ← Standalone ROUGE evaluation
├── requirements.txt    ← All dependencies
├── setup.sh            ← One-shot environment setup
├── data/
│   └── booksummarization.csv
├── saved_model/        ← Fine-tuned model (after training)
├── outputs/            ← Checkpoints during training
└── logs/               ← TensorBoard-compatible logs
```

---

## Quick Start

```bash
# 1. Set up environment
bash setup.sh

# 2. Train the model
python train.py

# 3. Summarize a book (text)
python inference.py --text "Old Major, the old boar on the Manor Farm…" --title "Animal Farm"

# 4. Summarize a text file
python inference.py --file my_book.txt --title "My Book"

# 5. Summarize a PDF
python inference.py --pdf my_book.pdf --title "My Book"

# 6. Summarize + generate audio
python inference.py --text "…" --tts --tts_backend gtts

# 7. Run evaluation report
python evaluate_model.py --model_path saved_model --num_samples 200
```

---

## Dataset

**CMU Book Summary Dataset** (Bamman & Smith, 2013)
- 16,559 book plot summaries from Wikipedia
- Tab-separated: `id | book_id | title | author | date | genres | summary`
- Average summary length: ~430 words
- Long summaries (up to 10,000 words) handled via **chunking**

---

## Chunking Strategy

Books can be very long. The system handles this with **overlapping token windows**:

```
Text: [-----chunk1------]
                [-----chunk2------]
                        [-----chunk3------]
      ←── stride ──►←─── overlap ───►
```

- `chunk_size = 450 tokens` (safe below BART's 512 limit)
- `overlap = 50 tokens` (prevents losing cross-boundary context)
- `merge_strategy = "concat"` or `"recursive"` (recursive = summarize the summaries)

---

## Training Configuration

| Parameter | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Base model | `facebook/bart-base` |
| Max input length | 512 tokens |
| Max target length | 128 tokens |
| Epochs | 3 |
| Train/Val split | 90/10 |
| Effective batch size | 16 (4 × 4 accumulation) |
| Learning rate | 3e-5 |
| Warmup ratio | 6% |
| FP16 | Yes (if GPU available) |
| Early stopping | Patience 3 |
| Best model selection | ROUGE-2 |

---

## Expected ROUGE Scores

After 3 epochs on the CMU dataset (target = first 80 words of plot):

| Metric | Expected Range |
|--------|---------------|
| ROUGE-1 | 42–48 |
| ROUGE-2 | 18–24 |
| ROUGE-L | 38–44 |

> Note: These scores reflect the self-supervised "headline extraction" proxy.
> With human-written reference summaries (e.g. GoodReads), ROUGE would typically be lower.

---

## Text-to-Speech Integration

Two backends are supported:

### Online (gTTS — Google TTS)
```python
from inference import SummaryToSpeech
tts = SummaryToSpeech(backend="gtts", language="en")
tts.speak("The farm animals revolted against their human masters.", "summary.mp3")
```

### Offline (pyttsx3)
```python
tts = SummaryToSpeech(backend="pyttsx3")
tts.speak("Summary text here.", "output.mp3")
```

### Full pipeline
```python
from inference import BookSummarizer, SummaryToSpeech

summarizer = BookSummarizer("saved_model")
summary = summarizer.summarize(long_book_text, title="Animal Farm")

tts = SummaryToSpeech(backend="gtts")
tts.speak(summary, "animal_farm_summary.mp3")
```

---

## Upgrading to BART-Large

For better quality (requires ~16GB GPU VRAM):
1. In `train.py`, set `model_name = "facebook/bart-large-cnn"` — already fine-tuned on CNN/DM
2. Reduce `train_batch_size` to 2 and increase `gradient_accumulation_steps` to 8
3. Lower `learning_rate` to 1e-5 for fine-tuning from bart-large-cnn

